The rewrite below reads the value with a single `memcpy` when the field's byte size equals `sizeof(T)` on a little-endian host. That replaces the per-byte shift/OR loop, which remains as the fallback for size mismatches and big-endian hosts. `GetType()` is now called once instead of twice. The result is unchanged:

- **Little-endian byte assembly:** the original loop builds the value in little-endian order, so on a little-endian host a direct `memcpy` produces the same bit pattern.
- **Signed `T`:** the bits come out the same, so values match too.
- **Out-of-range index:** the invalid-value return is unchanged.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T FieldBase::GetValue(const FIT_UINT8 fieldArrayIndex) const
{
    const FIT_UINT8 typeNum = GetType() & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK;
    const FIT_UINT8 size = baseTypeSizes[typeNum];
    if (fieldArrayIndex >= (FIT_UINT8)values.size())
    {
        return *reinterpret_cast<const T*>(baseTypeInvalids[typeNum]);
    }

    const FIT_UINT8* src = values.data() + (fieldArrayIndex * size);

#if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && defined(__ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__)
    if (size == sizeof(T))
    {
        T rv;
        std::memcpy(&rv, src, sizeof(T));
        return rv;
    }
#endif

    T rv = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
    {
        rv |= static_cast<T>(src[i]) << (i * 8);
    }

    return rv;
}
```

